WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:05.190
Love him or hate him, there's no denying that
Adam Sandler knows how to entertain his fans.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.656
And Netflix couldn't be happier with the results.

00:00:08.016 --> 00:00:13.120
Since the company started producing its own
content in 2011, Netflix has sought out partnerships

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.950
with a variety of established producers, writers,
directors, and actors.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.460
Many of these professionals are well-known
and respected within their industry, but that

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.320
clout doesn't always translate to audience
interest or attention.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.590
The likes of Denzel Washington, Bong Joon
Ho, Charlie Kaufman, and Steven Soderbergh

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.910
have made movies for Netflix.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.320
Celebrities such as Will Smith and Charlize
Theron have headlined major projects for the

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.740
streamer as well.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:40.489
But even more so than film auteurs and action
stars, Adam Sandler is a brand unto himself.

00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:44.030
Sandler has accumulated his fanbase over the
course of more than 30 years.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.770
The comedian first became a household name
on "Saturday Night Live" where he was a cast

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.760
member from 1990 to 1995.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.329
In short order, "SNL" launched Sandler into
a lucrative film career that began with 1995's

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:59.220
"Billy Madison" and continues to this day
with his Netflix deal.

00:00:59.596 --> 00:01:01.205
"You want a piece of me?"

00:01:01.898 --> 00:01:03.590
"I don't want a piece of you.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.350
I want the whole thing!"

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:11.283
He's prolific, with more than 80 credits to
his name, and a few of his films are genuine classics.

00:01:11.283 --> 00:01:15.164
One could argue that no "SNL" alum has had
Sandler's staying power.

00:01:15.164 --> 00:01:19.570
Stars like Eddie Murphy, Mike Myers, and Will
Ferrell may have had higher highs, but they've

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.290
all fallen off for long periods and they've
struggled to maintain their leading man status

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:25.140
in more recent years.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.530
Sandler is remarkably hard-working and consistent,
which is probably why Netflix was so eager

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:32.920
to bring him into their fold and is so happy
to keep him on board.

00:01:33.227 --> 00:01:36.830
Sandler is no doubt best known for portraying
the doofus everyman.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:40.960
In truth, there's not much difference between
Bobby Boucher and Hubie Dubois, and his fans

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.079
are fine with that.

00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:45.950
Lowbrow comedies that are basically "SNL"
style sketches drawn out to 90-ish minutes

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.930
account for the bulk of his résumé.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:51.710
And when he's not affecting a goofy accent
or involved in some form of hijinks, he often

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:56.330
stars in more traditional romantic comedies
or family movies like "50 First Dates," "Click,"

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:57.330
and "Pixels."

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:01.670
But while Sandler continues to make his traditional
comedy fair, he's also busy turning in praiseworthy

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:04.340
performances in awards-caliber films.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.300
Many of these still qualify as comedies, though
some are significantly more highbrow, not

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:09.780
to mention dark.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:14.610
Sandler's first foray into serious acting
came in Paul Thomas Anderson's 2002 dramedy

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:15.610
"Punch-Drunk Love."

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.000
That performance earned him a Golden Globe
nomination.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.071
He received even more accolades for his role
as Howard Ratner in 2019's "Uncut Gems," though

00:02:23.071 --> 00:02:25.920
he was snubbed for a widely expected Oscar
nod.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.800
Beyond those critically praised roles and
over the top comedies, he's occasionally toned

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:31.370
things down in his movies.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:36.280
This includes films for Netflix, like 2017's
"The Meyerowitz Stories (New and Selected)."

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.390
Most recently, he co-produced and led the
well-liked sports dramedy "Hustle" for the

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:42.080
streamer and, again, was a fixture on the
awards circuit.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.120
In fact, in 2023, he was presented with the
Mark Twain Prize for American Humor.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.510
Netflix surely wants Sandler for his signature
Happy Madison laugh riots, but it doesn't

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:55.000
hurt that he can also deliver powerhouse performances
in more grounded roles.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.470
"Hey, do you love this game?

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:58.790
I love this game.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:00.612
I live this game."

00:03:00.932 --> 00:03:04.702
What does well in movie theaters has certainly
changed since the early days of Adam Sandler's

00:03:04.702 --> 00:03:05.702
film career.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.549
In the 1990s, studio comedies regularly topped
box office charts.

00:03:09.549 --> 00:03:14.200
"Wayne's World," "Pretty Woman," "Ace Ventura:
Pet Detective," Sandler's own "The Waterboy,"

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:17.819
and "There's Something About Mary," were all
among the top 20 grossing films in the years

00:03:17.819 --> 00:03:19.290
of their releases.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.670
These days, it's unusual for a comedy of any
sort to crack that ceiling.

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:24.950
None managed it in 2022.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:29.040
The Julia Roberts and George Clooney-led throwback
"Ticket to Paradise" was considered a triumph

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.790
when it managed to break into the top thirty.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:35.459
By and large, comedies live on streaming platforms
now, and Netflix is an industry leader in

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:39.129
premiering lighthearted, non-IP fare directly
onto audiences' TVs.

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:43.140
There are several reasons for this and why
Sandler's movies in particular do so well

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.060
as a result of this shift in viewing habits.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.640
Theatrical showtimes have been eaten up by
sprawling franchises like the MCU and the

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:54.129
"Avatar" series in part because customers
can justify paying for what feels like an event.

00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:56.200
There's no rush to see a comedy.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.642
They can't really be spoiled.

00:03:57.642 --> 00:04:00.371
The visual effects aren't usually central
to the plot.

00:04:00.371 --> 00:04:03.690
And while laughing as part of a group can
enhance the experience, that can still be

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:08.170
accomplished with friends and family in a
living room for the price of a monthly subscription.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:11.909
Sandler's comedies are plentiful and broadly
appealing for those merely looking for something

00:04:11.909 --> 00:04:13.545
fun to watch on Netflix.

00:04:14.014 --> 00:04:18.250
Studio comedies disappeared from cinemas in
part because all mid-budget movies seemed

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.419
to vanish from the theatrical landscape.

00:04:20.419 --> 00:04:23.970
This occurred because big-budget franchise
installments and low-budget horror flicks

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.620
started to squeeze them out.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:31.560
Just last year, "Top Gun: Maverick" made nearly
$1.5 billion on a $170 million budget, while

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.770
the indie creep-fest "Skinamarink" made about
$2 million but cost only $15,000.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:42.100
As of late, a $40 or $50 million comedy anchored
by a once-bankable A-lister seems like a gamble,

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:43.440
if not a losing bet.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.610
So, slightly smaller-scale studio films have,
on the whole, migrated to streaming.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.729
Netflix has signed several nine-digit multi-picture
agreements.

00:04:51.729 --> 00:04:56.820
They forked over about $450 million for just
two of Rian Johnson's "Knives Out" sequels,

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:01.669
so Sandler's original deal, which guaranteed
him $250 million for four films, is a bargain

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:02.880
by comparison.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:06.500
No details have been released about a new
commitment between Sandler and Netflix.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:10.580
But comedies like his with a price tag of
roughly $60 million have, so far, clocked

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:12.630
in over 2 billion hours of viewing time.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.970
It must be a good return on CEO Ted Sarandos'
investment.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:22.169
Even projects that utterly miss the mark,
like 2015's spoof "The Ridiculous 6" are still

00:05:22.169 --> 00:05:26.922
a low-risk, high-reward proposition both for
Netflix and for Sandler's Happy Madison production company.

00:05:26.922 --> 00:05:30.606
"Well I have the microphone and you don't.

00:05:30.606 --> 00:05:35.389
So you will listen to every damn word I have
to say!"

00:05:35.854 --> 00:05:40.160
Moviegoers may have noticed that the length
of movies has been inching ever upward, especially

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:41.510
in the last few years.

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:46.100
Not that long ago, a three-hour-plus runtime
was extremely unusual, reserved only for the

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:48.449
occasional high fantasy or historical epic.

00:05:48.449 --> 00:05:54.587
But last year, "RRR," "Babylon," and "Avatar:
The Way of Water," all surpassed the 180-minute mark.

00:05:54.587 --> 00:05:59.880
Many more, including Steven Spielberg's autobiographical
family drama "The Fabelmans" and biopics "Blonde"

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.820
and "Elvis," hovered around two and a half
hours in length.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:06.370
And Netflix's own "The Irishman" is a whopping
209 minutes long.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:08.229
That can be daunting.

00:06:08.229 --> 00:06:11.440
There's no question that patrons want to get
their money's worth out of a ticket, but there's

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:14.250
a limit to audiences' free time and attention
span.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:17.981
Home viewing makes snacks and bathroom breaks
a little easier, but still, when subscribers

00:06:17.981 --> 00:06:21.640
are browsing for something to watch, there's
less of a barrier when the runtime is cut

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:22.525
in half.

00:06:22.525 --> 00:06:27.234
Sandler's trademark comedies tend to fall
somewhere between the 90- and 120-minute mark.

00:06:27.234 --> 00:06:31.680
On the shorter end, the "Murder Mystery" movies
are a mere 97 and 90 minutes.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:37.080
"Hubie Halloween" goes a breezy 103, and the
rest, "The Ridiculous 6," "The Do-Over," "The

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:40.980
Week Of," "The Meyerowitz Stories" and "Hustle,"
all come in under two hours.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:45.638
That means even if home viewers aren't thrilled
with their choice, it won't be a slog to get through.

00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:50.160
And if you wasted most of your night indecisively
scrolling through Netflix's infinite content

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.160
lineup, there's probably still time to fit
in an Adam Sandler movie.

00:06:53.575 --> 00:06:57.440
Just as consumers' habits have evolved at
the movie theater, they've also evolved at

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:58.660
home too.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:02.950
In the last year, streaming has surpassed
cable as the way most customers watch content.

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:06.419
One consequence of this significant paradigm
shift is that people no longer flip through

00:07:06.419 --> 00:07:08.139
channels to find something to watch.

00:07:08.139 --> 00:07:11.880
Nor do they leave whatever happens to be on
the TV as background noise.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:16.495
These days, many viewers seek out specific
titles, particularly on the day of a new streaming release.

00:07:16.495 --> 00:07:19.205
However, subscribers don't always want to
be so intentional.

00:07:19.205 --> 00:07:24.069
Subscribers still use their TVs the way they
always have, as a means to…chill out.

00:07:24.069 --> 00:07:28.277
Netflix has plenty of artistically ambitious
and thought-provoking original films to choose from.

00:07:28.277 --> 00:07:32.485
"Beasts of the Southern Wild," "Roma," and
"All Quiet on the Western Front" are just a few.

00:07:32.623 --> 00:07:35.440
But viewers aren't always in the mood for
something that requires them to put down their

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:40.710
phones and stay engaged, and definitely not
something that's academically or emotionally challenging.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:43.780
Sometimes, audiences want a movie they can
tune in and out of.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:47.860
Though the streamer has been desperate to
win a best picture Oscar and improve its critical

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:53.074
reputation, Netflix's customers love the platform
because of its accessible, binge-able, low-effort properties.

00:07:53.074 --> 00:07:55.184
Most Sandler comedies fit that bill.

00:07:55.263 --> 00:08:00.000
Even the more tightly plotted "Murder Mystery
2" doesn't require the brain power of "Glass Onion."

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.300
When it comes to how subscribers commonly
use the service, Sandler and Netflix's laid-back,

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:06.370
hang-out vibes are a perfect match for each
other.

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:07.390
Six degrees of Adam Sandler

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:11.360
The entertainment industry isn't as star driven
as it once was, but audiences still like to

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.050
see their favorites on screen.

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.630
It's even better when they're paired up together.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:18.770
If his friends and co-workers are to be believed,
Sandler is one of the most humble and down-to-earth

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:20.660
celebrities working today.

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:24.110
Because he's easy to work with, other big-name
actors and directors are happy to get involved

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:26.220
in whatever he's got going on.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:30.449
Sandler regulars like David Spade and Kevin
James tend to pop up in most of his projects,

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:32.740
which is a clear indicator of how much he
inspires loyalty.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:36.659
But he also brings along old pals like Jennifer
Aniston and Chris Rock.

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:40.849
Celebrated directors and producers have conceived
films with the comedian in mind too, which

00:08:40.849 --> 00:08:43.300
is a luxury not many actors enjoy.

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:46.580
Paul Thomas Anderson considered himself an
"obsessive" Sandler fan.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:51.006
And so he challenged himself to make an arthouse
version of a Sandler comedy with "Punch-Drunk Love."

00:08:51.085 --> 00:08:54.600
Though it's not currently on Netflix in the
United States, the connection between the

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.110
director and actor that developed because
of that film gave Sandler some impressive

00:08:58.110 --> 00:08:59.270
indie cred.

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:03.360
Later, Sandler teamed up with writer-director
Noah Baumbach for Netflix's "The Meyerowitz

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:06.459
Stories," which co-stars Ben Stiller as his
brother.

00:09:06.459 --> 00:09:11.200
Then, in 2022, Sandler got some of the best
reviews of his career for Netflix's "Hustle."

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.330
He co-produced the basketball dramedy with
LeBron James.

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:17.660
In the end, millions of Adam Sandler fans
seek out his movies on Netflix.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:21.660
But, because he has such a variety of famous
co-stars and collaborators, their fans end

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.250
up seeking out his movies as well.

00:09:23.517 --> 00:09:27.600
Despite his intermittent critical acclaim
and the fleeting brilliance of ideas like

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:31.310
"The Chanukah Song," Adam Sandler probably
doesn't come to mind when film buffs think

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:33.000
about the world's best actors.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:34.700
And that's part of his appeal.

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:38.070
He's the type of performer who understands
his strengths and understands exactly how

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:39.840
to push himself when he chooses to.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:44.149
Where Sandler has truly excelled in his 30-plus-year
career is as a producer.

00:09:44.149 --> 00:09:47.640
Not only has he figured out a filmmaking formula
that's practically market and critic-proof,

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:50.810
but he's actually ahead of the curve when
it comes to trends.

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:55.116
He's proven himself extremely savvy about
the types of movies he makes and when he releases them.

00:09:55.116 --> 00:09:59.190
A perfect example is when The first "Murder
Mystery" dropped on Netflix roughly six months

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:00.190
before "Knives Out."

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:04.190
He released the entertaining whodunit at exactly
the right time before the much bigger movie

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:05.360
premiered in theaters.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:09.671
He also headlined the emotional Noah Baumbach
family drama "The Meyerowitz Stories" two

00:10:09.671 --> 00:10:12.870
years before Adam Driver did the same in "Marriage
Story."

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:17.880
And he released his well-regarded sports movie
"Hustle" before 2023's "Air," "Champions"

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:18.880
and "Challengers."

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:22.320
He diversifies his portfolio more than it
might seem.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.680
Sandler has lent his voice to animated movies
like the "Hotel Transylvania" franchise.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:29.940
And he had the business acumen to make one
of his many broad appeal comedies, "Hubie

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:31.310
Halloween," a holiday offering.

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:35.080
It practically assures repeat yearly viewing
as a result.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:38.839
The unassuming actor, writer, and producer
continues to conquer streaming because he

00:10:38.839 --> 00:10:41.928
grasps how best to reach and grow his audience
using Netflix.

